The '''2024 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2023–24 UEFA Europa League]], the 53rd season of Europe's secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 15th season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was held at the [[Aviva Stadium]] in [[Dublin]], Ireland, on 22 May 2024, between Italian club [[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]] and German club [[Bayer 04 Leverkusen|Bayer Leverkusen]].<ref name="ExCo">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/026b-12c542441355-1c2040c5e54b-1000/ |title=Venues appointed for club competition finals |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 July 2021 |access-date=16 July 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://editorial.uefa.com/resources/0279-163596ae271e-e04faa20e08e-1000/20220926_circular_2022_65_en.zip |title=International match calendar and access list for the 2023/24 season |number=65/2022 |magazine=UEFA Circular Letter |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=26 September 2022 |access-date=27 September 2022}}</ref>

[[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]] won the match 3–0 for their first European trophy and their first trophy in 61 years courtesy of a hat-trick by [[Ademola Lookman]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/028d-1af3bf5e7e68-7d9202792002-1000/ |title=Atalanta 3–0 Leverkusen: Lookman treble brings UEFA Europa League glory to Bergamo |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=22 May 2024 |access-date=22 May 2024}}</ref> In doing so, he became the first hat-trick scorer in a Europa League final, the first in a major European final since [[Jupp Heynckes]] in the [[1975 UEFA Cup final#Second leg|1975 UEFA Cup final second leg]] and the first in a single-match major European final since [[Pierino Prati]] in the [[1969 European Cup final]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://iffhs.com/en/news/ademola-lookman-in-the-top-10-3636 |title=Ademola Lookman in the Top 10 !|date=23 May 2024|publisher=[[IFFHS]]|work=IFFHS.com|access-date=23 May 2024}}</ref> Atalanta also became the 30th different club to win the UEFA Cup/Europa League, the first Italian club since [[Parma Calcio 1913|Parma]] in [[1999 UEFA Cup final|1999]] to win the trophy and the first Italian side to win the competition since its rebranding before the [[2009–10 UEFA Europa League|2009–10 season]]. Leverkusen, who had been [[European association football club records and statistics#Longest unbeaten run across all competitions|unbeaten in 51 matches across all competitions]] in the season, became the first German side to lose a UEFA Cup/Europa League final since [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] in [[2009 UEFA Cup final|2009]].

As winners, Atalanta earned the right to play against [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], the winners of the [[2023–24 UEFA Champions League]], in the [[2024 UEFA Super Cup]]. Since Atalanta had already qualified to enter the league phase of the [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League]] through their league performance, the access list was rebalanced.

==Background==
This was [[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]]'s first ever European final. Having lost the [[2024 Coppa Italia final]], Atalanta failed to win a [[Double (association football)#European cup double|cup double]], but were still seeking a first major title since winning the [[1962–63 Coppa Italia]]. For [[Bayer 04 Leverkusen|Bayer Leverkusen]], this was their third ever European showpiece match,  the first European final since losing the [[2002 UEFA Champions League final]], and the first UEFA Cup/Europa League final since winning it in [[1988 UEFA Cup final|1988]]. Having won the [[2023–24 Bundesliga]] title and secured passage to the [[2024 DFB-Pokal final]], Leverkusen were aiming for a [[Treble (association football)|treble]].<ref name="UEL final preview">{{cite web|url= https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/028d-1ae2c2237b23-23b2339ec1ea-1000--europa-league-final-preview-atalanta-vs-leverkusen-where-t/|title=Europa League final preview: Atalanta vs Leverkusen – where to watch, kick-off time, possible line-ups, form guide|date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=22 May 2024}}</ref>

The final was only the third time the teams had met. Their first two matches were in the [[2021–22 UEFA Europa League knockout phase#Round of 16|2021–22 UEFA Europa League round of 16]]. Atalanta won both legs, [[2021–22 UEFA Europa League knockout phase#Atalanta v Bayer Leverkusen|3–2]] at home and [[2021–22 UEFA Europa League knockout phase#Bayer Leverkusen v Atalanta|1–0]] in the away game.<ref name="UEL final preview"/>

