===Atalanta===
{{see also|2023–24 Atalanta BC season}}
[[File:Scamacca (cropped).jpeg|thumb|upright=.8|[[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]] forward [[Gianluca Scamacca]] was the top scorer in the competition for his club with 6 goals leading up to the final.]] 

Atalanta qualified for the Europa League group stage by finishing 5th in the [[2022–23 Serie A]]. They were drawn into Group D alongside [[2022–23 Primeira Liga|Primeira Liga]] fourth-place team [[Sporting CP]], [[2022–23 Austrian Football Bundesliga|Austrian Football Bundesliga]] second-place team [[SK Sturm Graz|Sturm Graz]], and reigning [[2022–23 Ekstraklasa|Ekstraklasa]] champions [[Raków Częstochowa]].<ref name="GS draw">{{cite web|title=UEFA Europa League group stage draw|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/draws/2024/2001762/|date=1 September 2023|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In Atalanta's opener of the group stage, they faced Raków Częstochowa at the [[Stadio Atleti Azzurri d'Italia|Stadio di Bergamo]] and won 2–0, with goals from [[Charles De Ketelaere]] and [[Éderson (footballer, born 1999)|Éderson]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Atalanta-Raków|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039208/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 2, Atalanta defeated Sporting CP away from home at the [[Estádio José Alvalade]] 2–1, with goals by [[Giorgio Scalvini]] and [[Matteo Ruggeri]], and a goal from [[Viktor Gyökeres]] for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web|title=Sporting CP-Atalanta|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039216/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 3, Atalanta drew in a 2–2 stalemate at the [[Liebenauer Stadium|Stadion Graz Liebenau]] against Sturm Graz.<ref>{{cite web|title=Sturm Graz-Atalanta|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039231/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 4, Atalanta defeated Sturm Graz in the reverse fixture at the Stadio di Bergamo, with a goal from [[Berat Djimsiti]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Atalanta-Sturm Graz|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039255/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 5, Atalanta drew 1–1 with Sporting CP at home, with a goal from [[Gianluca Scamacca]] and [[Marcus Edwards]] for the visitors.<ref>{{cite web|title=Atalanta-Sporting CP|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039262/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 6 at the [[Zagłębiowski Park Sportowy]], Atalanta grabbed a 4–0 away win against Raków Częstochowa, with two goals from [[Luis Muriel]], and goals by [[Giovanni Bonfanti]] and Charles De Ketelaere.<ref>{{cite web|title=Raków-Atalanta|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039289/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In the round of 16, Atalanta were drawn against Sporting CP, a rematch from the group stage clash. In the first leg at the Estádio José Alvalade, Atalanta drew 1–1, with goals from Gianluca Scamacca and [[Paulinho (footballer, born November 1992)|Paulinho]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Sporting CP-Atalanta|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040315/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> In the second leg, Atalanta defeated Sporting CP 2–1, with goals from [[Ademola Lookman]] and Gianluca Scamacca cancelling out [[Pedro Gonçalves]]' opener to win 3–2 on aggregate and advance to the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web|title=Atalanta-Sporting CP|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040323/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Atalanta were drawn against English club and tournament favourites [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]. In the first leg, Atalanta upset the English team 3–0 away at [[Anfield]], with a brace from Gianluca Scamacca and a goal from [[Mario Pašalić]], ending Liverpool's 34-game unbeaten run at home across all competitions.<ref>{{cite web|title=Liverpool-Atalanta|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040517/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> In the second leg, Atalanta lost 1–0 with a goal from [[Mohamed Salah]], but the win in the second leg allowed Atalanta to advance 3–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|title=Atalanta-Liverpool|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040521--atalanta-vs-liverpool/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Atalanta were drawn against French club [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]]. In the first leg at the [[Stade Vélodrome|Stade de Marseille]], Atalanta drew 1–1, with goals from Gianluca Scamacca and [[Chancel Mbemba]] for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web|title=Marseille-Atalanta|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040524/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> In the second leg at the Stadio di Bergamo, the hosts defeated Marseille 3–0, with goals from Ademola Lookman, Matteo Ruggeri, and [[El Bilal Touré]]. Atalanta won 4–1 on aggregate to qualify for their first European final.<ref>{{cite web|title=Atalanta-Marseille|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040526/|access-date=23 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

===Bayer Leverkusen===
{{see also|2023–24 Bayer 04 Leverkusen season}}
[[File:Los Caminos del fútbol. Xabi Alonso (39666778464) (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=.8|[[Bayer Leverkusen]] manager [[Xabi Alonso]] led his team to the [[European association football club records and statistics#Longest unbeaten run across all competitions|longest unbeaten run across all competitions]] since the introduction of UEFA club competitions (1955–56), with a record of 51 matches unbeaten en route to the final.]] 

Bayer Leverkusen qualified for the Europa League group stage by finishing 6th in the [[2022–23 Bundesliga]], due to [[RB Leipzig]] winning the [[2022–23 DFB-Pokal]] while also qualifying for the [[2023–24 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] based on league position, meaning the Europa League place was passed down to sixth place. Leverkusen were drawn into Group H alongside reigning [[2022–23 Azerbaijan Premier League|Azerbaijan Premier League]] champions [[Qarabağ FK|Qarabağ]], [[2023 Eliteserien|Eliteserien]] fifth-place team [[Molde FK|Molde]], and [[2023 Allsvenskan|Allsvenskan]] third-place team [[BK Häcken]].<ref name="GS draw"/>

In Leverkusen's opener of the group stage, they faced BK Häcken at the [[BayArena]] and won 4–0, with goals from [[Florian Wirtz]], [[Amine Adli]], [[Victor Boniface]] and [[Jonas Hofmann]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Leverkusen-Häcken|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039200/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 2, Leverkusen defeated Molde away from home at the [[Aker Stadion]] 2–1, with goals by [[Jeremie Frimpong]] and [[Nathan Tella]], and a goal from [[Emil Breivik]] for the hosts.<ref>{{cite web|title=Molde-Leverkusen|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039224/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 3, Leverkusen were victorious again in a 5–1 thumping at home against Qarabağ.<ref>{{cite web|title=Leverkusen-Qarabağ|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039241/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 4, Leverkusen defeated Qarabağ in the reverse fixture at the [[Tofiq Bahramov Republican Stadium]], with a goal from Victor Boniface.<ref>{{cite web|title=Qarabağ-Leverkusen|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039249/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 5, Leverkusen won 2–0 against BK Häcken at [[Ullevi]], with goals from Victor Boniface and [[Patrik Schick]] for the visitors.<ref>{{cite web|title=Häcken-Leverkusen|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039273/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> On matchday 6 at the BayArena, Leverkusen grabbed a 5–1 win against Molde, with two goals from [[Adam Hložek]], and goals by Patrik Schick and [[Edmond Tapsoba]] as well as an [[Own goal#Association football|own goal]] by [[Martin Ellingsen]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Leverkusen-Molde|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2039280/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In the round of 16, Leverkusen were drawn against Qarabağ, a rematch from the group stage clash. In the first leg at the Tofiq Bahramov Republican Stadium, Leverkusen drew 2–2, with goals from Florian Wirtz and Patrik Schick.<ref>{{cite web|title=Qarabağ-Leverkusen|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040317/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> In the second leg, Leverkusen defeated Qarabağ 3–2, with one goal from Jeremie Frimpong and two late goals from Patrik Schick cancelling out goals from [[Abdellah Zoubir]] and [[Juninho (footballer, born 1996)|Juninho]] to win 5–4 on aggregate and advance to the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web|title=Leverkusen-Qarabağ|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040325/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Leverkusen were drawn against English club and reigning [[2022–23 UEFA Europa Conference League|Europa Conference League]] winners [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]]. In the first leg, Leverkusen produced a 2–0 home win at BayArena, with two late goals coming from Jonas Hofmann and Victor Boniface.<ref>{{cite web|title=Leverkusen-West Ham|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040518/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> In the second leg, the match ended with a 1–1 draw, where despite [[Michail Antonio]] opened the scoring early for West Ham, Jeremie Frimpong equalised late on to ensure that the two goal advantage from the first leg was enough for Leverkusen to advance 3–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|title=West Ham-Leverkusen|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040522/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Leverkusen were drawn against Italian club [[AS Roma|Roma]], runners-up of the [[2022–23 UEFA Europa League|previous edition]]. In the first leg at the [[Stadio Olimpico]], Leverkusen won 2–0, with goals from Florian Wirtz and [[Robert Andrich]] enough for the away team to carry an advantage into the second leg.<ref>{{cite web|title=Roma-Leverkusen|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040525/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> In the second leg at the BayArena, the hosts came from 2–0 down after 66 minutes, courtesy of a brace from [[Leandro Paredes]], before scoring two goals late on via an own goal from [[Gianluca Mancini]] and a 97th minute goal from [[Josip Stanišić]] to draw the match 2–2. Leverkusen won 4–2 on aggregate and progressed to the Europa League final, while also setting a new European unbeaten record of 49 successive games, surpassing the [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] side that was managed between December 1963 and February 1965.<ref>{{cite web|title=Leverkusen-Roma|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2040527/|access-date=22 May 2024|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

==Pre-match==

===Identity===
The original identity of the 2024 UEFA Europa League final was unveiled at the group stage draw on 1 September 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |last=UEFA.com |date=2023-09-01 |title=2024 UEFA Europa League and UEFA Europa Conference League final identities revealed |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0285-18df8e6abdf2-f54ceb925270-1000--2024-uefa-europa-league-and-uefa-europa-conference-league-fi/ |access-date=2023-09-02 |website=UEFA.com|language=en}}</ref>

===Ambassador===
The ambassador for the final was former Irish international [[John O'Shea]] who walked onto the pitch with the trophy before the match.<ref>{{cite web | last=McDonnell | first=Daniel | title=John O'Shea in the dark over Ireland manager job but he doesn't expect it will be him | website=Irish Independent | date=17 April 2024 | url=https://www.independent.ie/sport/soccer/international-soccer/john-oshea-in-the-dark-over-ireland-manager-job-but-he-doesnt-expect-it-will-be-him/a1009168623.html | access-date=10 May 2024}}</ref>

===Ticketing===
From a capacity of 48,000 seats, 36,000 were made available to the fans and the general public, of which both competing team's supporters received an allocation of 12,000 tickets each.<ref>{{cite web | title=Ticket sales for 2024 men's UEFA club competition finals and UEFA Super Cup launched | website=UEFA.com| date=9 April 2024 | url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/028c-1a9c5b793851-c86ab42d20c8-1000--ticket-sales-for-2024-men-s-uefa-club-competition-finals-an/ | access-date=22 May 2024}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:2019-10-23 Fußball, Männer, UEFA Champions League, RB Leipzig - FC Zenit St. Petersburg 1DX 2641 by Stepro.jpg|thumb|upright=.8|[[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]] forward [[Ademola Lookman]] was named [[Player of the match#Association football|man of the match]] after becoming the first person to score a [[Hat-trick#Association football|hat-trick]] in a Europa League final.]]

In the 12th minute, Atalanta went in front after [[Teun Koopmeiners]] played a [[Passing (association football)#Through pass|through-ball]] to [[Davide Zappacosta]] who then played the ball low across from the right where [[Ademola Lookman]] was lurking, and the Nigerian managed to run in front of [[Exequiel Palacios]] and strike the ball into the top left-hand corner of the net to give the Italian side the lead. Lookman doubled Atalanta's lead fourteen minutes later, after collecting the ball and [[Dribbling#Association football|dribbling]] past [[Granit Xhaka]] before striking his effort into the bottom right-hand corner of the net to make it 2–0.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.theguardian.com/football/article/2024/may/22/atalanta-bayer-leverkusen-europa-league-final-match-report|title=Atalanta win Europa League as Lookman hat-trick ends Leverkusen's unbeaten run|date=22 May 2024|work=The Guardian|accessdate=23 May 2024}}</ref>
Leverkusen came close to getting a goal back after [[Álex Grimaldo]] was played through on goal by Palacios, but the Spaniard's attempted [[Chip (association football)|chip]] ended up safely in the hands of [[Juan Musso]]. Atalanta pushed for a third as the game progressed, with [[Charles De Ketelaere]] having an effort saved by [[Matěj Kovář]], and [[Piero Hincapié]] forced to prevent a [[Cross (association football)|cross]] from Koopmeiners from reaching De Ketelaere. In the 75th minute, Lookman completed his [[Hat-trick#Association football|hat-trick]] after receiving the ball from [[Gianluca Scamacca]] on the left before managing to find himself space and get a shot away, which ended up in the top right-hand corner of the net. The match ended with Atalanta winning 3–0, as they secured their first ever European title.<ref name="BBC Report">{{cite news |title=Atalanta vs Bayer Leverkusen |url= https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/cw005j1704lt#Report |access-date=21 May 2024 |newspaper=BBC Sport}}</ref>

